PRSL-AI ECOSYSTEM
PRIVACY & SECURITY
Canonical Definition v1.0

Organization: Robert Software Labs
Status: Canonical
Classification: Foundational / Cross-Cutting Security Layer
Architecture: Human-Centered / Privacy by Default / Local-First / AI-Agnostic

==================================================

1. PURPOSE

PRSL-AI Privacy & Security defines how the PRSL-AI Ecosystem protects the user's identity, data, context, memory, knowledge, communications, capabilities, devices and actions.

Privacy & Security is a cross-cutting architectural capability.

It does not belong exclusively to one subsystem.

It applies across:

- Identity.
- Context.
- Memory.
- Knowledge.
- Intelligence.
- Governance.
- Integrations.
- iRiel System.
- Devices.
- Storage.
- AppWeb.
- Intelligence Server.
- Intelligent Presence.
- Resonance.

==================================================

2. CORE DEFINITION

Privacy defines how information should be collected, processed, retained, shared and controlled.

Security protects that information and the systems responsible for processing it against unauthorized access, modification, disclosure, misuse or destruction.

Canonical distinction:

Privacy
→ Should this data be used, exposed or retained?

Security
→ How do we protect it from unauthorized access or manipulation?

Both operate together.

==================================================

3. ARCHITECTURAL POSITION

Privacy & Security operates transversally across the ecosystem.

Canonical model:

                     PRSL-AI
                        │
              Privacy & Security
             /    /    |    \    \
            ↓    ↓     ↓     ↓    ↓
        Context Memory Knowledge Intelligence
            ↓    ↓     ↓     ↓
              Governance
                   ↓
              iRiel System
                   ↓
                Devices

Privacy & Security is not an optional endpoint.

It establishes boundaries throughout the lifecycle of information and actions.

==================================================

4. FOUNDATIONAL PRINCIPLE

Privacy is a default state.

Security is a continuous requirement.

The system must not assume that data should be:

- Collected.
- Transmitted.
- Stored.
- Remembered.
- Shared.
- Exposed.

The system must establish necessity and authorization first.

==================================================

5. PRIVACY BY DEFAULT

PRSL-AI follows Privacy by Default.

Canonical behavior:

Minimize
↓
Classify
↓
Process locally when possible
↓
Restrict access
↓
Transmit only when necessary
↓
Protect transmission
↓
Retain only when justified
↓
Delete when no longer required

Privacy must not depend on:

- Subscription.
- Device price.
- AI provider.
- User technical expertise.
- Application tier.

==================================================

6. LOCAL-FIRST

When a capability can safely and effectively operate locally, local processing is preferred.

Canonical preference:

Local
↓
Authorized Personal Infrastructure
↓
External Infrastructure

Cloud processing is not prohibited.

Unnecessary cloud processing is discouraged.

==================================================

7. DATA MINIMIZATION

PRSL-AI should process the minimum information required to accomplish the intended task.

Canonical principle:

Necessary data
↓
Necessary component
↓
Necessary purpose
↓
Necessary duration

More data does not automatically produce better intelligence.

==================================================

8. DATA CLASSIFICATION

Information may be classified according to sensitivity.

Example classification:

PUBLIC
↓
PERSONAL
↓
PRIVATE
↓
SENSITIVE
↓
HIGHLY SENSITIVE

Classification influences:

- Storage.
- Processing.
- Access.
- Transmission.
- Retention.
- Logging.
- Authorization.

==================================================

9. SENSITIVE DATA

Sensitive information may include:

- Health information.
- Intimate images.
- Financial information.
- Authentication credentials.
- Private communications.
- Precise location.
- Biometric information.
- Personal identifiers.
- Private documents.
- Personal behavioral information.

Highly sensitive information receives stronger controls.

==================================================

10. USER AUTHORITY

The user remains the primary authority over their personal information.

The ecosystem should support:

- Visibility.
- Consent where appropriate.
- Access control.
- Modification.
- Portability.
- Deletion.
- Controlled sharing.

The system must not treat personal information as unrestricted infrastructure.

==================================================

11. IDENTITY PROTECTION

Identity must be separated from device ownership.

Canonical relationship:

User
↓
Identity
↓
Personal Intelligence Environment
↓
Devices

A device is an endpoint.

It is not the definition of the user's identity.

==================================================

12. ACCESS CONTROL

Access must be based on:

- Identity.
- Authorization.
- Scope.
- Capability.
- Context.
- Governance.
- Purpose.

Possessing technical access must not automatically imply permission to use data.

==================================================

13. LEAST PRIVILEGE

Every component should receive the minimum privileges required for its responsibility.

Example:

An Integration that needs calendar access should not automatically receive:

- Health data.
- Private messages.
- Financial information.
- Long-term Memory.
- Unrelated Knowledge.

Capability access should remain purpose-specific.

==================================================

14. ISOLATION

Sensitive information should be isolated according to:

- User.
- Identity.
- Application.
- Capability.
- Scope.
- Security boundary.

A compromised component should not automatically compromise the entire Personal Intelligence Environment.

==================================================

15. ENCRYPTION

Sensitive information should use appropriate cryptographic protection.

Protection should apply where appropriate to:

- Data at rest.
- Data in transit.
- Credentials.
- Authentication material.
- Sensitive local storage.
- External communication.

Encryption is necessary but not sufficient.

==================================================

16. ENCRYPTION ≠ PRIVACY

Encrypted transmission does not automatically make data handling appropriate.

The system must first determine:

Should this data leave the device?

Then:

If necessary, how should it be protected?

Canonical order:

Necessity
↓
Authorization
↓
Minimization
↓
Encryption
↓
Transmission

==================================================

17. EXTERNAL DATA TRANSMISSION

When data must leave the local environment:

1. Determine necessity.
2. Classify sensitivity.
3. Minimize the payload.
4. Apply policy.
5. Verify authorization.
6. Encrypt transmission.
7. Restrict destination.
8. Control retention.

Highly sensitive information should not be transmitted silently.

==================================================

18. EXTERNAL AI PROVIDERS

External AI providers are treated as processing capabilities, not as owners of the ecosystem.

Before sending sensitive information externally, the system should evaluate:

- Necessity.
- Provider.
- Data sensitivity.
- User authorization.
- Governance policy.
- Security.
- Retention.
- Applicable contractual or regulatory requirements.

==================================================

19. AI-AGNOSTIC SECURITY

Privacy & Security must not depend on a specific AI provider.

The security boundary exists before provider selection.

Canonical flow:

User Data
↓
Privacy / Security
↓
Governance
↓
Provider Selection
↓
AI Processing

Not:

User Data
↓
Provider
↓
Privacy

==================================================

20. CONTEXT PRIVACY

Context may contain highly relevant personal information.

Context must therefore be:

- Scoped.
- Temporary when appropriate.
- Access-controlled.
- Minimized.
- Protected from unnecessary propagation.

Current relevance does not imply permanent retention.

==================================================

21. MEMORY PRIVACY

Memory is persistent by nature and therefore requires stronger controls.

Sensitive information should not automatically become Memory.

Memory should respect:

- Purpose.
- Authorization.
- Retention.
- User control.
- Deletion.

==================================================

22. KNOWLEDGE PRIVACY

Knowledge may contain information originating from private sources.

Knowledge must preserve:

- Source.
- Scope.
- Permissions.
- Provenance.
- Sensitivity.

Private Knowledge must not become globally accessible merely because it has been indexed.

==================================================

23. INTELLIGENCE PRIVACY

Intelligence should receive only the information required for reasoning.

Canonical principle:

The model does not automatically receive everything the ecosystem knows.

Information should be selected according to:

- Intent.
- Context.
- Authorization.
- Purpose.
- Sensitivity.

==================================================

24. iRIEL SYSTEM SECURITY

Execution requires authorization.

Canonical flow:

Intent
↓
Capability
↓
Authorization
↓
Governance
↓
Execution
↓
Verification
↓
Result

Security must prevent unauthorized execution even when an instruction appears valid.

==================================================

25. INTEGRATION SECURITY

Integrations are trust boundaries.

An integration must not receive unrestricted access to the ecosystem.

Canonical model:

Integration
↓
Capability
↓
Permission
↓
Required Data
↓
Operation
↓
Result

A compromised integration should remain confined to its authorized scope.

==================================================

26. DEVICE SECURITY

iRiel Devices are security boundaries for local computation and storage.

Devices should protect:

- Identity.
- Local Context.
- Memory.
- Knowledge.
- Credentials.
- Private files.
- Communications.
- Intelligence state.

Device storage is part of the privacy architecture.

==================================================

27. STORAGE SECURITY

Storage should follow the data's sensitivity and lifecycle.

Not all information requires identical:

- Retention.
- Encryption.
- Replication.
- Accessibility.
- Backup.
- Deletion policies.

Intelligent Storage Lifecycle must therefore respect Privacy & Security policies.

==================================================

28. TEMPORARY DATA

Temporary information should remain temporary whenever possible.

Examples:

- One-time image analysis.
- Temporary document processing.
- Short-lived Context.
- One-time API responses.

Temporary processing must not automatically become:

Memory
or
Knowledge.

==================================================

29. RETENTION

Data retention should have an explicit purpose.

Possible states:

TEMPORARY
AUTHORIZED RETENTION
MEMORY
KNOWLEDGE
ARCHIVED
DELETED

Retention should be minimized for sensitive information.

==================================================

30. DELETION

The ecosystem should support controlled deletion of personal information.

Deletion must consider:

- Active Context.
- Memory.
- Knowledge.
- Local storage.
- Cached representations.
- External processing where applicable.
- Derived indexes.

Deletion should not merely remove the visible interface representation while leaving uncontrolled copies.

==================================================

31. AUDITABILITY

Security-relevant actions should provide appropriate auditability.

Possible events:

- Authentication.
- Authorization.
- Permission changes.
- Sensitive access.
- External transmission.
- Execution.
- Security failures.
- Policy decisions.

Auditability must itself respect privacy.

The system should not create unnecessary surveillance through logging.

==================================================

32. OBSERVABILITY

The ecosystem must remain observable enough to diagnose failures and security events.

However:

Observability
≠
Unlimited collection.

Logs should minimize personal and sensitive information.

==================================================

33. SECURITY INCIDENTS

If a security boundary is violated or suspected:

- Isolate the affected component.
- Restrict access.
- Preserve necessary evidence.
- Prevent propagation.
- Notify appropriate control layers.
- Recover securely.
- Re-evaluate affected credentials or permissions.

A compromised integration should not automatically compromise unrelated components.

==================================================

34. FAILURE BEHAVIOR

Security failures must fail closed where appropriate.

Possible states:

ALLOWED
DENIED
RESTRICTED
UNKNOWN
UNAVAILABLE
FAILED

Unknown authorization should not be interpreted as permission.

==================================================

35. PRIVACY FAILURE

A privacy failure may include:

- Unnecessary transmission.
- Unauthorized access.
- Excessive retention.
- Unintended exposure.
- Cross-user leakage.
- Unnecessary data collection.
- Improper memory creation.

Privacy incidents must be treated as architectural failures, not merely UI problems.

==================================================

36. SECURITY FAILURE

A security failure may include:

- Unauthorized access.
- Credential compromise.
- Integrity violation.
- Privilege escalation.
- Data exfiltration.
- Unauthorized execution.
- Boundary bypass.

Security controls should limit both probability and impact.

==================================================

37. HUMAN-CENTERED SECURITY

Security must remain understandable and accessible to the user.

Security mechanisms must not become so complex that users cannot understand what the system is doing.

The user should be able to understand:

- What is happening.
- Why it is happening.
- What data is involved.
- Whether external processing occurs.
- What permission is required.
- What happened after execution.

==================================================

38. ACCESSIBILITY AND PRIVACY

Accessibility is part of privacy protection.

A blind user, for example, must receive equivalent information about:

- Data transmission.
- Permission requests.
- Security states.
- Execution results.
- Failures.

Privacy information must be available through accessible channels.

==================================================

39. TRUST

PRSL-AI does not ask the user to trust the system blindly.

Trust must emerge from:

- Consistent behavior.
- Transparency.
- Privacy.
- Security.
- Verification.
- User control.
- Predictable boundaries.

Canonical principle:

Trust is demonstrated, not requested.

==================================================

40. ZERO-TRUST ORIENTATION

The architecture should not assume that every component is trustworthy simply because it belongs to the ecosystem.

Trust should be evaluated according to:

- Identity.
- Capability.
- Authorization.
- Scope.
- Context.
- Policy.

Internal components should also respect security boundaries.

==================================================

41. PRIVACY + GOVERNANCE

Governance establishes enforceable policy.

Privacy defines the protection objective.

Security provides technical enforcement.

Canonical relationship:

Philosophy
↓
Privacy Principles
↓
Governance
↓
Security Controls
↓
Implementation

==================================================

42. PRIVACY + ACCESSIBILITY

The user's vulnerability must never reduce their privacy.

Accessibility must never require unnecessary exposure of personal information.

The system should provide equivalent protection regardless of interaction modality.

==================================================

43. PRIVACY + INTELLIGENCE

Intelligence should receive the minimum information necessary to perform the requested task.

The most powerful model should not automatically receive the most data.

Capability does not justify unrestricted access.

==================================================

44. PRIVACY + MEMORY

Memory must be treated as a privileged persistence mechanism.

The ecosystem must distinguish:

"Observed"

from:

"Stored permanently."

Sensitive information requires heightened retention controls.

==================================================

45. PRIVACY + KNOWLEDGE

Knowledge must preserve access boundaries.

Information being indexed does not make it public.

Knowledge retrieval must respect:

- Identity.
- Scope.
- Permission.
- Provenance.
- Sensitivity.

==================================================

46. PRIVACY + INTEGRATIONS

An integration should receive only the information required for the operation it performs.

Example:

Calendar capability
→ Calendar information.

Not:

Calendar capability
→ Entire Personal Intelligence Environment.

==================================================

47. PRIVACY + DEVICES

iRiel Devices provide the preferred local environment for sensitive processing when capabilities permit.

Local-first processing reduces unnecessary exposure but does not eliminate the need for device security.

==================================================

48. DAILY-LIFE EXAMPLE — BANKING

User:

"iRiel, how much money do I have available?"

Preferred flow:

Bank Integration
↓
Secure authentication
↓
Required financial value
↓
Local processing
↓
Response

The entire financial history should not be exposed to an AI model if it is unnecessary.

==================================================

49. DAILY-LIFE EXAMPLE — PRIVATE MESSAGES

User:

"What did I agree with João yesterday?"

The system should retrieve only the relevant information.

It should not expose the user's complete messaging history to an external intelligence provider unnecessarily.

==================================================

50. DAILY-LIFE EXAMPLE — LOCATION

User:

"What's the fastest route home?"

Location may be processed for the requested operation.

The request does not automatically authorize permanent storage of the user's precise location history.

==================================================

51. DAILY-LIFE EXAMPLE — INTIMATE IMAGE

User:

"iRiel, I noticed something unusual and took a photo."

The image should be classified as highly sensitive.

Preferred behavior:

Local processing
↓
No automatic Memory
↓
No automatic Knowledge
↓
No unnecessary transmission
↓
Accessible explanation
↓
User-controlled next step

==================================================

52. DAILY-LIFE EXAMPLE — MEDICAL DOCUMENT

User:

"Explain this examination result."

The document should be treated as sensitive health information.

The system should:

- Protect the document.
- Process locally when possible.
- Minimize external transmission.
- Communicate uncertainty.
- Avoid unsupported diagnosis.
- Avoid automatic retention.

==================================================

53. DAILY-LIFE EXAMPLE — EXTERNAL AI

If external processing is required:

Sensitive data
↓
Classification
↓
Minimization
↓
Authorization
↓
Encrypted transmission
↓
Controlled processing
↓
Controlled result

Not:

Sensitive data
↓
Automatic upload.

==================================================

